WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.780
Jeff Kitchen, Author/Screenwriter/Instructor:
These 36 dramatic situations are every element

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:10.730
that is in any story anywhere and a lot of
people said that's insane.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:13.700
I could come up with way more and they tried
to come up with a list and they couldn't even

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:14.980
get to 24.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.760
They were like actually it's better than I
thought.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.500
Film Courage: Jeff can you give us an overview
of the 36 dramatic situations?

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.279
Jeff: The 36 dramatic situations is really
a brainstorming tool.

00:00:28.279 --> 00:00:38.129
It works with elements that are common in
storytelling like madness, disaster, ambition,

00:00:38.129 --> 00:00:39.929
those types of things.

00:00:39.929 --> 00:00:46.809
It was created by an Italian playwright named
Carlo Gozzi in the 1700s and it was endorsed

00:00:46.809 --> 00:00:51.339
by Gerta and Schiller and on the strength
of those endorsements that hung around for

00:00:51.339 --> 00:00:58.629
about 150 years until a guy named George Poulty
turned it into a book in 1916. and then it

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:06.979
picked up some steam again but it's it's unknown
by many people and i've met a lot of people

00:01:06.979 --> 00:01:15.439
who said they had the book but could never
make heads or tails out of it it's it's basically

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:25.670
what Gozzi said was that all stories consist
of these basic 36 human emotional conflicts

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:31.880
and there are things like ambition madness
conflict with a god the necessity of sacrificing

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:41.929
loved ones and it's very similar in nature
to the periodic table of elements in chemistry

00:01:41.929 --> 00:01:48.600
where you know carbon hydrogen all that stuff
so with the periodic table of elements you

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:54.280
could describe anything in this room quite
completely from one point of view like this

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:00.700
table is carbon hydrogen oxygen whatever all
the elements that are in it you can describe

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:05.460
it quite completely from that one point of
view that's only one point of view but that's

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.190
getting right at the elements that are in
it and in storytelling it's the same thing

00:02:10.190 --> 00:02:17.290
where you can say okay this story has madness
conflict with a god vengeance taken for kindred

00:02:17.290 --> 00:02:27.680
upon kindred and there's different so different
active elements in any given story which is

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:37.650
useful because it helps you when you're starting
out with a story where you can like for instance

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:43.420
let's look at your derrick story idea again
so we'd be looking at what would be let me

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:48.959
just get my book here because

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:57.400
it's easy to go down the one sheet oh great
for these so here's a list of the here's a

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:06.329
list of the 36 dramatic situations just listed
simply so looking at it in terms of Derek's

00:03:06.329 --> 00:03:16.030
story so deliverance rescuing or being rescued
crime pursued by vengeance pursuit disaster

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:23.090
revolt daring enterprise the enigma the riddle
or the mystery obtaining madness he may be

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:28.329
like losing his mind as being pressed into
this dilemma into like what do I do here i'm

00:03:28.329 --> 00:03:32.959
in love with her she's offering me a life
of adventure but I have to betray my most

00:03:32.959 --> 00:03:39.750
core values but are those core values based
on something that were real or was I snookered

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:46.430
into guarding criminals I don't know i'm losing
my mind these are all just story possibilities

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:51.920
some of it is like some of what I was doing
were elements that are already active in the

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:58.379
story idea before it's even touched you want
to see what you've got it's like taking inventory

00:03:58.379 --> 00:04:02.920
like what are the elements that are already
active in it and there may be a lot more than

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:13.159
you thought there were which is good self-sacrifice
for an ideal all sacrificed for a passion

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:18.640
the necessity of sacrificing loved ones is
gonna have to give her up rivalry of superior

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:24.910
and inferior that's always a good one because
it's so subjective of like who's the superior

00:04:24.910 --> 00:04:32.100
and who's the inferior and it's completely
point of view sensitive and it can even flicker

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:38.060
back and forth of who has the upper hand in
any given situation discovery of the dishonor

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:43.530
of a loved one if he's finding out that she
is you know a criminal obstacles to love is

00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.530
very active in this story but sit before it's
even touched and enemy loved falling in love

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:56.120
with someone who turns out to be your enemy
or enemy love can be fascination with an enemy

00:04:56.120 --> 00:05:02.220
or coming to understand your enemy or falling
in love with your enemy that kind of thing

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:09.250
ambition is very central in this story because
Derek is stuck in this rut and he has ambitions

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.940
for more but he doesn't know how to get there
and she may awaken ambition in him or he may

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:20.650
have a curious lack of ambition like why don't
I care that i'm stuck in this rut or why do

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:26.890
I care that she's dragging me down this weird
road so it can be the very absence of one

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:31.020
of these situations like you if you look at
a character's ambition it may be that their

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.440
ambition is wildly out of proportion and getting
them in trouble or they could have utterly

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:41.930
no ambition which is out of proportion for
a given situation it's curious i'm like why

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:47.810
does this person genuinely not care which
way they go is so it's like it can be the

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:53.550
presence or absence of these things too that
can be suggestive these are really suggestive

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:58.770
elements for brainstorming so you can see
that some of what i'm doing here is as i'm

00:05:58.770 --> 00:06:04.710
going down the list and i'm thinking about
this story it's helping with the what if i'm

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.520
seeing what is already there before the story
was ever even touched the elements that it

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:23.000
showed up with and what if his ambition to
forge a wildly new life for himself with her

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:31.070
is something that explodes in him like he's
realizing how dead his life had been before

00:06:31.070 --> 00:06:37.010
because he was just even though he was dissatisfied
with the job in many ways he was still just

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:45.210
nose to the grindstone numb plotting ahead
and he's like how did I ever live like that

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:49.660
for years and years I couldn't go back to
another second of that i'd blow my brains

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:56.860
out it's like yeah she's trouble but I can't
go back to nothing I can't go back to an emotional

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:03.960
vacuum anymore I kind of stuffed myself into
that to forge ahead but I don't fit in that

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:07.500
room anymore

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:12.960
conflict with a god that's always interesting
because it doesn't necessarily have to have

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:18.410
any kind of connotation any kind of religious
connotation it conflict with god suggests

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:24.690
a religious connotation but it's just any
time there's something going up against a

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:31.400
bigger power it could be you versus the neighborhood
bully it could be trying to get you know something

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:43.330
done against city hall so that it's these
are very flexible the less literally you take

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:48.580
these the more actual use you can make make
of them so you want to have a poetic or metaphoric

00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:54.460
frame of mind as you look at these you don't
want to be stuck with their actual definitions

00:07:54.460 --> 00:08:02.570
at all if you look at one of these things
even if it's not a valid chain of logic that

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:07.500
you look at one of these things and it gives
you 10 ideas those 10 ideas are what's important

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.690
this is just a catalyst in chemistry a catalyst
is something that you throw into a chemical

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:19.050
mix and it creates a reaction even though
the original catalyst stays the same so even

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:25.020
if this triggers makes you think of something
that happened to you as a kid which makes

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:30.590
you think of a weird possibility for your
story which triggers dynamic possibilities

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:35.120
for the ending you've been stuck on it doesn't
matter how this triggered a chain of thought

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:40.939
that got you there their job is to throw gasoline
on the fire and trigger stuff and that's that's

00:08:40.939 --> 00:08:47.860
its only function really it's just a brainstorming
tool it's just to expose you to the elements

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:51.890
that are already active in your story even
if you hadn't noticed that already and to

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:57.110
expose you to possibilities that you wouldn't
have thought of in 20 lifetimes things that

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:01.769
you never would never have occurred to you
that all of a sudden are like wow that could

00:09:01.769 --> 00:09:06.519
really tweak my third act i've been dying
for something like this you know it's just

00:09:06.519 --> 00:09:12.161
like it's it's throwing hand grenades into
your story idea

00:09:12.161 --> 00:09:19.089
erroneous judgment is very central in this
story look how big it is before you touch

00:09:19.089 --> 00:09:29.000
anything because he's like he's looking at
her going am I crazy or is she like the most

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:35.149
exciting thing in the history of mankind or
am I like ready for a freaking straight jacket

00:09:35.149 --> 00:09:40.730
and like her too you know it's like I don't
even know which way my head screwed on anymore

00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:46.390
and like the things that i'm contemplating
doing with her and overturning my job and

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:50.920
committing treason and blah blah I was like
I don't know whether to shoot myself or run

00:09:50.920 --> 00:09:57.160
off with her and have 20 kids like I don't
know what i'm thinking anymore recovery of

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:04.070
a lost one is he going to get his own sense
of self back loss of loved ones is he going

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:15.230
to lose her is he losing himself is he losing
his sense of honor so these are like

00:10:15.230 --> 00:10:19.649
you want to have a really flexible frame of
mind when you look at them because you'll

00:10:19.649 --> 00:10:23.749
get so much more out of them

00:10:23.749 --> 00:10:29.639
it's like the wrench doesn't have to fit the
bolt head exactly if it'll turn it you know

00:10:29.639 --> 00:10:33.279
even if it kind of fits and it'll turn it
that's all you need you're just trying to

00:10:33.279 --> 00:10:41.279
advance your story or to see more deeply into
it because you're penetrating into the inner

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:48.529
elements that constitute your story this tool
is really useful when you're starting a story

00:10:48.529 --> 00:10:56.260
from scratch because say you're riding on
a bus and you hear the two people in front

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:01.089
of you talking and it's a whacked out conversation
and you're kind of listening in because it's

00:11:01.089 --> 00:11:06.100
so interesting and one of them says something
and you're like that would make an amazing

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:10.620
screenplay holy crap you rush home and you're
like boom oh I gotta you just go on a mile

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:15.749
a minute that's a great time to go to the
36 dramatic situations because you're exploding

00:11:15.749 --> 00:11:21.279
with ideas and if you look down the list of
the 36 you'd be like that's already in there

00:11:21.279 --> 00:11:28.579
that's already in there I didn't know that's
in there but boy is that potent and could

00:11:28.579 --> 00:11:35.660
that be in there that would really whack it
out in a completely dangerous way or make

00:11:35.660 --> 00:11:42.589
it more hilarious or whatever the story is
going it's like god that is pure dynamite

00:11:42.589 --> 00:11:48.060
and it's like your head's already exploding
with ideas and you're tossing explosive elements

00:11:48.060 --> 00:11:53.610
you're playing mad scientist with explosive
elements and you may hit one of them and you're

00:11:53.610 --> 00:12:03.319
like that can't be or could it you'd be like
that's scary that's truly terrifying I i don't

00:12:03.319 --> 00:12:07.660
even dare think then but you can't get away
from me you're like

00:12:07.660 --> 00:12:13.370
okay well what if I did do that you're like
so it may trigger 100 ideas it's like one

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:17.970
of them can give you a cascade of ideas like
you can barely write them all down you're

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:24.139
already cascading with ideas and this is throwing
gasoline on the fire you're throwing explosive

00:12:24.139 --> 00:12:32.579
elements into the mix playing what if and
so if that one idea gives you 10 ideas and

00:12:32.579 --> 00:12:38.829
each of those gives you 10 ideas get all that
stuff down on paper that's what's important

00:12:38.829 --> 00:12:46.079
that thing doesn't mean anything it just got
you thinking so it's a extremely valuable

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:51.660
brainstorming tool in part because you're
being exposed to one complete spectrum of

00:12:51.660 --> 00:13:03.430
ideas because where the idea originally came
from Gozzi said this these 36 dramatic situations

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:11.051
are every element that is in any story anywhere
and a lot of people said that's insane I could

00:13:11.051 --> 00:13:14.029
come up with way more and they tried to come
up with a list and they couldn't even get

00:13:14.029 --> 00:13:15.060
to 24.

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.819
they were like actually it's better than I
thought and then Schiller and Gerta both said

00:13:19.819 --> 00:13:28.480
this is amazing and so it just hung around
and it's it's just a tool it's just one complete

00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:37.120
spectrum of ideas that you can whip out and
use to trigger ideas for your story and to

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:43.870
see elements in it that you hadn't noticed
so that you're going wow this idea was deeper

00:13:43.870 --> 00:13:49.339
and more complex than I thought at first glance
there's more going on there than I noticed

00:13:49.339 --> 00:13:55.250
at first which is great because then you've
got better material you've got more to work

00:13:55.250 --> 00:14:02.350
with if you're doing a tv series you're like
exploding with seasons and you know cliffhangers

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:07.670
and characters these things can suggest characters
they can suggest resolution they can complicate

00:14:07.670 --> 00:14:16.050
dilemmas but it's just one complete spectrum
of possible ideas so it's not the be-all and

00:14:16.050 --> 00:14:23.579
end-all it's not the only place to look for
story ideas it's just one tool and not only

00:14:23.579 --> 00:14:31.290
is it useful when you're starting out on an
idea but it's useful to take a quick run through

00:14:31.290 --> 00:14:35.790
the one sheet every now and then just like
part way through your script you may go back

00:14:35.790 --> 00:14:40.790
and look through it and go a crime pursued
by vengeance that's emerging as a much more

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:46.689
substantial aspect of the story and I hadn't
really thought it through yet but so it can

00:14:46.689 --> 00:14:52.189
it can just shake things loose or help you
solve things you've been wrestling with or

00:14:52.189 --> 00:14:57.639
expose you to possibilities that might never
have occurred to you or help you see that

00:14:57.639 --> 00:15:04.519
this element is very active in the story if
you hadn't noticed it yet and then when you

00:15:04.519 --> 00:15:09.069
get stuck at different parts of the story
where you're really stuck on your third act

00:15:09.069 --> 00:15:15.279
or something going back through it can shake
things loose clarify things open up possibilities

00:15:15.279 --> 00:15:21.610
help you see things that are in it that you
hadn't noticed before that kind of thing so

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:30.600
it's a it's really an explosive brainstorming
tool and you want to have broad flexibility

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:38.060
with it a real sense of play not feel trapped
by any of it it doesn't dictate anything to

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:43.220
you it doesn't say this has to be in there
and even if at the beginning of the story

00:15:43.220 --> 00:15:48.529
you were like wow ambition is right at the
nucleus of this story by the time you've worked

00:15:48.529 --> 00:15:52.889
on it for a while you may come back and look
at it again and be like ambition doesn't even

00:15:52.889 --> 00:15:58.149
matter anymore it's like I thought that was
huge but it's grown and changed so much so

00:15:58.149 --> 00:16:03.059
you're not it's it's just

00:16:03.059 --> 00:16:10.129
it's just like getting blown in the air by
explosives at certain points in the development

00:16:10.129 --> 00:16:16.829
process to shake things loose trigger ideas
help crystallize things clarify things simplify

00:16:16.829 --> 00:16:24.519
things complicate things and that's all part
and parcel of the normal storytelling process

00:16:24.519 --> 00:16:30.230
anyway this is just some good chemicals to
throw in there that are like fertilizer or

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:39.420
clarifying agents or complicators or clarifiers
so you see it in super clarity all of a sudden

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:45.990
like it can take a while spend time with the
story for quite a while before you get true

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:55.870
clarity with it like Sydney Pollock when they
were working on Tootsie spent a long time

00:16:55.870 --> 00:17:02.190
trying to figure out what the story's really
